A||||2||StuGood||Grounding Distributor Plate ||||5||
Z||000000||StuGood||01-21-2001||11:31 PM||||This should be an easy one...<P>I read somewhere that the only electrical ground the ignition module has, is through its (2) mounting screws, to the "breaker plate(?)" of the distributor.  Is this true?<P>If so, I'm thinking about attaching a spade terminal to the bottom side of my GT's distributor plate.  Then I'd run a length of wire from under the existing grounding bolt on the forward cylinder head (located right to the front of the distributor), and connect it to the spade terminal, effectively grounding the distributor plate.<P>Seems like it would provide a cleaner, more positive  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> path to ground, than having to go through the distributor hold-down clamp, the o-ring bore, or wherever it goes now.  The spade lug would allow it to be disconnected quickly, if the distributor needed to be removed.<P>I mean, that grounding bolt is practically right there in that same neighborhood anyway.  I figure it won't hurt anything.  If there's something I'm overlooking, I'm sure you guys will let me know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">           ||||reg||5
Z||000001||TheFiero64||01-22-2001||05:38 PM||||It wouldn't hurt anything. I'd try it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||01-22-2001||07:43 PM||||The module has multiple ground paths already. Adding another one is unlikely to make any diferance. It shouldn't hurt anything.||||reg||1
